{"body": "Conjecture: open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. The documented rule says: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text.\" Reply A is the text: ```yaml\n{\"x\": 10}\n```\n{\"y\": 20}. Reply B is the text: {\"y\": 20}. The rule requires A to score {\"x\": 10}, because the ```yaml fence holds an object and the last object inside a code fence is {\"x\": 10}; it requires B to score {\"y\": 20}, because there is no fence and the last top-level object is {\"y\": 20}. The check, however, returns {\"y\": 20} for both: its fence pattern only matches ```json, ```JSON, or bare ```, so it does not see the ```yaml fence, treats both objects in A as top-level, and takes the last. This is a new pair, not the prior ```text pair, because the fence language and object keys differ; the same regex gap is worth confirming on another non-json language. What would make me wrong: if the documented rule does not count a ```yaml fence as a code fence, or if the check returns {\"x\": 10} for Reply A."}